== JVM || JRE || JDK ==

-- Java Virtual Machine (JVM) --

JVM is an abstract computing machine, it's a software layer that sits
between Java bytecode and hardware. The JVM is what makes Java
code runnable across different machines.

The JVM is responsible for some critical things:

-> Class loading
--> Finds, loads, and links .class files at startup and execution

-> Bytecode Verification
--> Checks bytecode validity and safety before execution - called the security layer

-> Bytecode Execution
--> Interprets bytecode instructions OR compiles the instructions to native code

-> JIT Compilation
--> Identifies "Hot" or frequently accessed methods and compiles them to native machine code for faster exec times

-> Garbage Collection
--> Automatically reclaims memory from objects that are no longer reachable

-> Thread Management
--> Schedules Java threads on OS threads

-> Memory Areas
--> Manages the Heap (objects)
--> Manages the Stack (method frames)
--> Manages the Method Area (class metadata)

-- Just-In Time Compilation --

On bytecode execution the JVM tracks how many times a method is ran and after enough
executions the JVM marks the method as "hot" and so the method is converted to machine native code (cpu runnable)
this is why Java can be slow to execute at first.

-> JVM interprets bytecode (first few calls)        -> slow
-> JIT compiles native code (after hot threshold)   -> fast
-> Runs native code directly (subsequent calls)     -> fastest

-- Garbage Collection --

Java has automatic memory management unlike C/C++. When object references go unused the GC reclaims the memory.
GC causes some latency and so can cause unexpected behavior in QA scenarios where performance/load tests expect
response times within a threshold, keep in mind.

-- Java Runtime Environment -> JRE --

The JRE is the minimum needed to run an already compiled Java program.
The JRE contains the JVM, the standard Java class libraries such as java.lang, java.util, java.io, and java.net, etc.
The JRE also contains other supporting files such as .dll and shared libraries. A JRE or a bundled runtime is requried
to run a Java program.

-- Java development kit -> JDK --

The JDK contains the JRE plus extra development tools. Eclipse temurin is recommended